# WP4-SIZING-amended-2: the layer share decides both branches and the maximum decides neither

Supersedes `WP4-SIZING-amended.patch`. Apply this one; it is the whole module and its tests.

## The pairing was wrong, and it is orderable rather than a matter of taste

`layer_share = sqrt(Σ span_h²) / sqrt(Σ total_h²)`, and `span_h ≤ m · total_h` termwise for `m` the
maximum per-head share. So **`layer_share ≤ max_head_share` at every layer, always.** Checked
against 200,000 random layers as well as derived: the largest observed `layer_share − max_share` is
exactly 0.

That ordering is what breaks the pairing I implemented. Negligibility on the maximum is strictly
stricter than on the layer share, and it routes the case it was meant to catch backwards. The
separating fixture — a loud head at 0.001 beside a quiet head at 0.9 — fails negligibility at
0.900, falls to the effect branch, is sized against a layer share of 0.00135, and comes back
**"not worth running"**, when the plan's rule applied to the quantity the plan names sizes against
0.05 and returns 217 points. The plan's "share at every layer" is the hidden span's contribution to
that layer's read, which is the layer share.

Both are now in the signature and neither can be mislabelled: `layer_share_by_layer` decides
negligibility and the licensed effect alike, `max_head_share_by_layer` is recorded and never sizes,
and `share_statistic` is gone because the pairing is structural and there is nothing left to label.
The two mappings must describe the same layers, or the pre-check was read in half.

## The maximum still earns its place

`single_head_dominates` is true when some layer's maximum reaches the negligible threshold while
its layer share does not — one head carrying the read, which is the case the maximum exists to
catch. The reader meets it without the sizing acting on it. A test separates dominance from a
merely large maximum: a layer at 0.4 with a maximum of 0.9 is not negligible either way and does
not raise the flag.

Which statistic decides negligibility stays the Head's to change, since issue 82 puts admissibility
with the Head. The pairing now sits in one place, so that is a one-line change with the choice
visible.

